From 5eee8017a827659b6fb9e29ab1241eb4227c56f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Denys Vlasenko <vda.linux@googlemail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 07:31:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ash: do not read past the text of an unset MAIL

Patch by Florian Schmaus <florian.schmaus@codasip.com>:

ash stores each variable as a single "NAME=value" string in var_text,
and accessor macros like mailval() simply skip over the "NAME=" prefix
(var_text + 5). Per the comment above them, they return the null
string for unset variables. That only works if an unset variable's
text still has a NUL where the value would start.

MAIL and MAILPATH are unset by default, and their initial texts are
"MAIL" and "MAILPATH". For "MAIL" (5 bytes including the NUL)
mailval() therefore points one byte past the end of the string. When
MAILPATH is not set, chkmail() falls back to mailval() and hands that
pointer to padvance_magic(), which parses the memory following "MAIL"
as a colon-separated list of mail files. This happens before every
interactive prompt, unless the user sets MAIL or MAILPATH.

What gets read there depends on the binary layout. In an x86_64
defconfig build "MAIL" is directly followed by "MAILPATH", so ash
stat()s ./MAILPATH before every prompt and prints "you have mail"
when a file of that name in the current directory changes. This
stat() of ./MAILPATH was already noticed in 2016 [1], and the
overread itself was recently reported after fuzzing ash with
AddressSanitizer [2].

The texts used to be "MAIL\0" and "MAILPATH\0", as they still are in
dash. Commit 8837c5dec402 ("ash: remove one redundant function,
improve string sharing, better field names") dropped the extra NUL
from all unset-by-default variables, so the literals become plain
strings the compiler can put in mergeable string sections. That is
fine for RANDOM, LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE and HISTFILE, whose values are only
read through lookupvar(), which checks VUNSET. But mailval() is
used without such a check.

Fix the out-of-bounds read by restoring the trailing NULs for MAIL
and MAILPATH, so that mailval() and mpathval() again yield "" for an
unset variable. This matches setvar(), which already allocates a
zeroed buffer of name length + 2 when unsetting a variable at
runtime. MAILPATH is not strictly affected, since mpathval() is only
used after mpathset(), but this keeps both accessors consistent.
Neither literal is shared with any other string, so the cost is two
bytes of .rodata:

function                                             old     new   delta
.rodata                                           102098  102100      +2

We ran into this on a CHERI [3, 4] system. CHERI's architectural
capabilities bound each memory region precisely, so it catches
out-of-bounds accesses that MMU-based protection misses. Here the
one-byte overread of the "MAIL" string literal is a capability length
violation, and every interactive ash dies with SIGSEGV on start-up.

1: https://github.com/gliderlabs/docker-alpine/issues/163
2: https://github.com/vda-linux/busybox_mirror/issues/47
3: https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/research/security/ctsrd/cheri/
4: https://codasip.com/solutions/riscv-processor-safety-security/cheri/

Signed-off-by: Denys Vlasenko <vda.linux@googlemail.com>

[Backported to 1.38.0 for LibreELEC Hades: the context of the varinit_data[]
hunk differs in 1.38.0; the change itself is the same.]
---
diff --git a/shell/ash.c b/shell/ash.c
--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -2252,6 +2252,7 @@
 #define VSTRFIXED       0x04    /* variable struct is statically allocated */
 #define VTEXTFIXED      0x08    /* text is statically allocated */
 #define VSTACK          0x10    /* text is allocated on the stack */
+//^^^ is VSTACK unused?
 #define VUNSET          0x20    /* the variable is not set */
 #define VNOFUNC         0x40    /* don't call the callback function */
 #define VNOSET          0x80    /* do not set variable - just readonly test */
@@ -2300,8 +2301,10 @@
 	 */
 	{ VSTRFIXED|VTEXTFIXED       , defifsvar   , NULL            },
 #if ENABLE_ASH_MAIL
-	{ VSTRFIXED|VTEXTFIXED|VUNSET, "MAIL"      , changemail      },
-	{ VSTRFIXED|VTEXTFIXED|VUNSET, "MAILPATH"  , changemail      },
+	// Two NULs are necessary since mailval()/mpathval() use var_text+5/+9
+	// to skip over var name and '=' sign (if variable is set).
+	{ VSTRFIXED|VTEXTFIXED|VUNSET, "MAIL\0"    , changemail      },
+	{ VSTRFIXED|VTEXTFIXED|VUNSET, "MAILPATH\0", changemail      },
 #endif
 	{ VSTRFIXED|VTEXTFIXED       , bb_PATH_root_path, changepath },
 	{ VSTRFIXED|VTEXTFIXED       , "PS1=$ "    , NULL            },
@@ -2651,6 +2654,7 @@
 		*p++ = '=';
 		strcpy(p, val);
 	}
+	// else: the string is "VARNAME\0\0". Important for var_text+5 to work correctly for "MAIL" et al
 	vp = setvareq(nameeq, flags | VNOSAVE);
 	INTON;
 
-- 
